    {
        "content": "<p>Is there a \"working mathematician's handbook\" to expressing these kinds of concepts in lean? Pre-LLM world I often stumbled at things like translating the sentence \"Consider the vector space spanned by elements of the group. We explicitly turn this into an algebra in such-and-such a fashion\".</p>\n<p>Of course, if you know how to read the docs you can just pull that \"out of thin air\" through <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=MonoidAlgebra#doc\">docs#MonoidAlgebra</a> and because instances are often elided its a bit invisible. More recently I had fun trying to define a different kind of multiplication on essentially the same type <code>(M →₀ R)</code>.</p>\n<p>Perhaps what I'm asking is if there is a more user-manual kind of document, like the \"Theories\" section at <a href=\"http://leanprover-community.github.io\">leanprover-community.github.io</a> particularly for algebraic structures.</p>",
        "id": 576831204,
        "sender_full_name": "Robert Spencer",
        "timestamp": 1772487123
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathematics_in_lean/\">#mil</a> is partially this, or at least something such a working mathematician is likely to want to have read through. It needs more chapters but I think a lot more are planned (and help I think is welcome to add more).</p>",
        "id": 576835732,
        "sender_full_name": "Julian Berman",
        "timestamp": 1772488871
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I feel like there is a wealth of resources for teaching how to prove theorems in Lean for beginners, but I'm having a hard time finding resources to do things like formalizing definitions in Lean.</p>",
        "id": 576946613,
        "sender_full_name": "Sarah El Boustany",
        "timestamp": 1772535837
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't understand how to write a definition from that example with inferInstance</p>",
        "id": 576947033,
        "sender_full_name": "Sarah El Boustany",
        "timestamp": 1772535944
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think the point is that often writing a definition makes it more difficult because there's another alias to go through. I'd rephrase what Kevin said as</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TensorProduct</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">enveloping_algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"bp\">ᵐᵒᵖ</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">enveloping_algebra</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- EnvelopingAlgebra.enveloping_algebra (k : Type) [CommRing k] (A : Type) [CommRing A] [Algebra k A] : Type</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- The inferrence can't seem to work out its a semiring</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SemiRing_enveloping_algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Semiring</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">enveloping_algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">inferInstanceAs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Semiring</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"bp\">ᵐᵒᵖ</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- it's already in the system when stated correctly</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">enveloping_algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">inferInstanceAs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"bp\">ᵐᵒᵖ</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Or you can prove it directly</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">enveloping_algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">smul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">algebraMap</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">commutes'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">smul_def'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n<span class=\"o\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
